WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.197
Secrets commonly leak through places that were intended to help troubleshooting: logs, exceptions, stack traces, telemetry, traces, health checks, crash dumps, screenshots, support bundles, debug pages, and diagnostic endpoints.

2
00:00:15.197 --> 00:00:21.396
These channels often have broader access and longer retention than the original secret store.

3
00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:24.795
Redaction and filtering reduce accidental exposure.

4
00:00:24.795 --> 00:00:35.526
Applications should avoid logging request headers, connection strings, tokens, keys, certificates, provider-specific credentials, and full configuration objects.

5
00:00:35.526 --> 00:00:44.991
Structured logging can capture safe categories, source names, validation outcomes, and correlation identifiers without printing secret values.

6
00:00:44.991 --> 00:00:51.589
Full configuration dumps are risky because sensitive values and ordinary settings sit side by side.

7
00:00:51.589 --> 00:00:59.188
Even if a dump is meant for authorized operators, it can be copied into tickets, chat, email, or support packages.

8
00:00:59.188 --> 00:01:05.653
Diagnostics should be designed to answer operational questions without revealing access material.

9
00:01:05.653 --> 00:01:09.186
Development and production diagnostics should differ.

10
00:01:09.186 --> 00:01:13.918
Developers may need more detail locally, using development-only values.

11
00:01:13.918 --> 00:01:19.983
Production should provide safe user messages, protected operator logs, and correlation IDs.

12
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:25.649
A development diagnostic feature should not become a production endpoint by accident.

13
00:01:25.649 --> 00:01:29.181
Support and troubleshooting workflows deserve review.

14
00:01:29.181 --> 00:01:36.713
Screen sharing, screenshots, exported logs, support bundles, and incident notes can all capture sensitive values.

15
00:01:36.713 --> 00:01:45.045
Teams should define what may be collected, how it is redacted, where it is stored, who can access it, and when it is deleted.

16
00:01:45.045 --> 00:01:47.911
Exposure prevention is not only about code.

17
00:01:47.911 --> 00:01:57.642
It is about habits, tooling, documentation, and operational guardrails that keep secrets from appearing in places where they cannot be controlled.
